NEWOS DOOM port - 0004-defaults-table-and-key-bindings

Reason: the defaults table stores int* and char** in the same slot, and
the released code funnels both through "int", which truncates every
pointer to 32 bits on x86_64. default_t becomes void* + long, and both
M_LoadDefaults() and M_SaveDefaults() pick the width from the magnitude
test the save path already used (string defaults are addresses, so they
are never near zero).

M_LoadDefaults() also parsed default.cfg with fscanf()/sscanf(), and the
NEWOS libc implements no scanf family at all: the parser becomes
line-at-a-time with strtol(base 0), accepting the same grammar.

Finally, the key bindings: SYS_KBD_READ delivers decoded bytes, so a
bare modifier never reaches userland and the upstream CTRL/ALT/SHIFT
scancode slots are unreachable. fire/strafe/speed move to the left-hand
home column, which the arrow keys leave free.

Apply from inside the extracted linuxdoom-1.10/ directory: patch -p1 < this-file

--- a/m_misc.c
+++ b/m_misc.c
